  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Tracing CSS Selectors**\n\nI\'m now meticulously dissecting the specified CSS selectors within the DOM structure. My focus is on understanding their precise targeting behavior. I\'m taking the first selector, div#root:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth, to understand precisely which elements are impacted. I\'ll extend my analysis to the remaining selectors after this first one, and compare to establish any overlap, or nuances.\n\n\n**Analyzing Selector Targets**\n\nI\'ve moved on to examining the remaining CSS selectors. I\'m currently tracing the DOM path from `div#root` to understand the nesting. It seems like the second selector targets the outer wrapper, and the third selects a form element within a specific nested `div`. I\'m verifying the selector\'s precision by checking the `ChatDetailView.tsx` component. This helps establish where each rule will apply within the component structure.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Element Hierarchy**\n\nI\'m now identifying the exact elements targeted within `ChatDetailView`. Specifically, I\'ve confirmed that the initial `div:nth-of-type(1)` selector within `ChatDetailView` selects the sticky bottom interactive area. I\'m focusing on the direct children of that div, to identify which child corresponds to `div:nth-of-type(1)`, the second CSS selector, and which is `form:nth-of-type(1)`, the third selector. I am noting the content for each direct child element.\n\n\n**Confirming Selector Specificity**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the exact element targeted by each selector. The first one selects the Quick Action Utilities Drawer. Now I see that the second one is a bar with quick reply pills. Then, the third is indeed the bottom input bar. They are all direct children of the sticky bottom interactive area.\n\n\n\n**Enhancing Pill Interactions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the quick reply pills. I want to make them more engaging. Currently, they only send a message. My plan is to add visual feedback\342\200\224a checkmark or a cycle of follow-up replies when a pill is clicked\342\200\224to make the interaction richer. Also, I\'m thinking of incorporating a realistic contextual auto-reply from the other party to simulate a more natural conversation flow.\n\n\n**Refining Voice Input**\n\nI am now focusing on the \'Voice Memo Button\' element within the bottom input bar. I\'m transforming it from a static element into a fully interactive voice input toggle. Clicking the mic will switch between text input and a \'hold to speak\' mode. This would launch a live voice recording animation with a duration counter and send a playable voice message bubble.\n\n\n**Expanding UI Interactivity**\n\nMy focus has shifted to enhancing the chat input and its related features. I\'m transforming the emoji button into a dynamic popover, to create an Emoji & Campus Sticker Popover. I\'ll make the plus button toggle the Quick Action Utilities Drawer, with the plus icon rotating to signal its status. Finally, the send button will now auto-scroll the chat stream and trigger a realistic reply from the seller.\n\n\n**Developing Image Sharing**\n\nI\'m now focused on the Quick Action Utilities Drawer\'s second div, specifically the photo-related buttons. I\'m moving beyond simple text responses. My goal is to create interactive elements. The first will become a Campus Photo Picker that sends a rich image message, while the second will allow live condition checks with photo cards.\n\n\n**Implementing Payment Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating face-to-face payment features. I\'m focusing on the \"Campus Face-to-Face Escrow\" sheet, incorporating item price, safety checks, and a payment confirmation button. Upon confirmation, I\'ll generate a \"Payment Completed Receipt Card\" within the chat stream. I am also working on `src/data/marketplaceData.ts` to add support for rich message types.\n\n\n"
