  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the UI Request**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the user\'s request. It seems they want improvements to the chat message interface. Specifically, I need to focus on optimizing the initial hidden state of the bottom drawer and resolving the mismatch in selected highlight styles. It sounds like there are usability issues and inconsistencies to iron out.\n\n\n**Refining the Initial State**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the initial state of the bottom drawer and the selected highlight display. The goal is a seamless user experience. I\'m focusing on the default hidden state of the drawer upon entering the chat view and how to elegantly manage its state during conversation switches and input focus events. The highlight styles, in the quick action utilities drawer and bottom input bar, require more focused refinement to make them look cohesive.\n\n\n**Improving Visual Consistency**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on visual consistency. The mismatched appearance of the `add_circle` button when the drawer was open, with the pink background and rotated plus, needs to be fixed. The original design had a cleaner look. I\'m aiming for subtle styling, potentially using `text-primary bg-surface-container` or clean `text-primary` for the icon state. Also, `\347\272\246\345\256\232\351\235\242\344\272\244` was permanently highlighted in the utilities drawer, and needs to be addressed.\n\n\n**Unifying Visual Language**\n\nI\'m now striving for visual harmony. The current design shows a clash between styles for selected tools in the drawer. I plan to use `selectedTool` to unify the look, applying consistent styling across the four items. When a tool is selected, I want to use a refined highlight on the icon box and label, which has been successful, but must be checked for the quick reply pills and voice/emoji toggles too. The goal is a cohesive, user-friendly interface that feels integrated.\n\n\n"
