  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Request**\n\nI\'m currently delving into the user\'s statement about incomplete features preventing publication. Simultaneously, I\'m parsing the request to apply style changes. I\'ve begun to consider the specific CSS selector provided, focusing on its structure and intended target, specifically `div#root:nth-of-type(1) > div:`. I\'m breaking down the selector\'s parts to understand its specificity.\n\n\n**Examining Element Location**\n\nI\'m now focused on the specific HTML element and its placement within the `WantedPlazaView.tsx` file. I see the target button is intended to be positioned at the bottom-right. The use of `right-5` causes an issue on desktop viewports. I\'m also considering the implications of the current rudimentary modal for the publish process. I\'m still trying to understand what the user wants to be added.\n\n\n**Reviewing Publication Process**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in the \"Post Wanted Request\" flow. I\'ve pinpointed crucial missing elements within the existing modal, focusing on `handleCreateWanted` in `WantedPlazaView.tsx`. The current modal lacks a category selector, leading to hardcoded categories. I\'m also finding an absence of quick template tags, which forces users to manually enter information and shows an error instead of letting them choose a template. Further investigation of `budgetMin`, `budgetMax`, and `recommendedPrice` is needed.\n\n\n**Addressing Budget and Features**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the hardcoded budget values \342\200\223 I need to implement proper parsing of the user\'s input for `budgetMin`, `budgetMax`, and `recommendedPrice`. It\'s clear that the current implementation is flawed. I\'ve also identified key missing features: a campus/pickup location selector, an urgency tag selector, and a department/identity selector. Additionally, I need to add a success feedback mechanism upon publishing, and adjust `selectedCategory`.\n\n\n**Designing New UI Flow**\n\nI\'m now sketching out a new user interface flow for posting wanted requests. I\'m focusing on a dedicated sheet/page with a rich experience, similar to the `PublishItemView`. I\'m envisioning one-click templates, categorized selection, budget input with number parsing, and pickup location options. I\'m also planning to implement quick title entry features and visual distinction of this new feature.\n\n\n**Implementing Item Publication**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the complete item publication flow. I must handle and present specific inputs like campus, location, and requirement tags and integrate them correctly. Upon publication, I will validate the information and create a `WantedItem` with the provided details. I will also incorporate an immediate feedback mechanism, displaying a confirmation banner and ensuring the published item is visible at the top of the list, along with an option to manage or withdraw it.\n\n\n"
