  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Component Hierarchy**\n\nI\'ve got an interesting observation! I noticed a peculiar rendering order issue. Specifically, I see a concerning scenario with the `BottomNavBar` component. It appears to be rendered after a modal within the `WantedPlazaView.tsx` component\'s DOM structure. This feels odd, and I am going to investigate more, specifically line 18 in `BottomNavBar.tsx` and lines 271 and 346 of `WantedPlazaView.tsx`.\n\n\n**Verifying Rendering Order**\n\nI\'ve confirmed my initial suspicion! The `BottomNavBar`, with its `z-50` and `fixed bottom-5` positioning, is indeed intercepting clicks on the \"\347\253\213\345\215\263\345\217\221\345\270\203\345\210\260\346\261\202\350\264\255\345\271\277\345\234\272\" submit button within the modal. This happens because the modal, despite also having a `z-50`, is rendered *before* the navbar in the DOM. This causes a user interaction issue, where the user may click the `BottomNavBar` instead of the intended button. This can be resolved with a reordering in the DOM or an adjusted z-index, and more importantly, by paying attention to the details of z-index and DOM order.\n\n\n**Improving User Experience**\n\nI\'ve got a crucial update. My focus shifted to improving the user experience for \"\345\217\221\346\261\202\350\264\255.\" I\'ve identified that the `BottomNavBar` was overlaying the \"\347\253\213\345\215\263\345\217\221\345\270\203\345\210\260\346\261\202\350\264\255\345\271\277\345\234\272\" button in the modal. Specifically, the CSS stacking order caused it to render on top of the submit button. Now, I\'m examining how to improve the `<aside>` floating button\'s functionality, ensuring it remains properly aligned across all devices. I\'m taking the \"\345\217\221\346\261\202\350\264\255\" flow to the quality of the `PublishItemView.tsx`!\n\n\n**Enhancing Modal Functionality**\n\nI\'m now focused on the \"\345\217\221\345\270\203\346\240\241\345\206\205\346\261\202\350\264\255\" experience and am working on improvements! I want to turn the modal into a rich, interactive sheet that sits above the `BottomNavBar` with a high `z-index` (z-[80]). My goal is to prevent any overlap on the submit button. I am considering providing quick templates based on common needs, such as a construction template, a dorm kitchen template, a book template and an accessories template. Also, I am going to have category pills.\n\n\n**Designing New Modal Content**\n\nMy latest thought process centers on enriching the modal content for the \"\345\217\221\345\270\203\346\240\241\345\206\205\346\261\202\350\264\255\" experience. I\'m focusing on key details, like validation and character count for the item title. For the budget, I\'m thinking about clear inputs for the minimum and maximum budget ranges with quick budget presets. I also want quick campus and location chips and tags for the user\'s specific department/grade. Finally, I\'m considering a text area with quick clickable tags, so the user can easily express requirements.\n\n\n**Implementing Post-Publish Behavior**\n\nI\'m now deep in the implementation. Clicking \"\347\253\213\345\215\263\345\217\221\345\270\203\345\210\260\346\261\202\350\264\255\345\271\277\345\234\272\" should provide a better user journey. If there\'s no title, I\'ll prompt the user with a 1-click option to populate a default example or highlight the input. Upon submission, a new `WantedItem` gets created with all the real data, and then it\'s prepended, `selectedCategory` resets, the modal closes, the user scrolls to the top, and a \"\345\217\221\345\270\203\346\210\220\345\212\237\" banner appears. Moreover, items from the current user will have a new badge and management actions! I am checking `src/data/marketplaceData.ts` to implement.\n\n\n"
