  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Budget Handling**\n\nI\'m currently working on refactoring the budget input handling within `src/data/marketplaceData.ts`. My focus is a new pure function, `parseBudgetRange` or potentially `createWantedItem`, to ensure precise and unit-consistent parsing of budget entries. This should cover all user input scenarios, from single values to range strings.\n\n\n**Analyzing `buildWantedItem`**\n\nI\'ve been tracing the execution flow of `buildWantedItem` function to understand its current behavior, now that it is tested. The function appears to handle budget inputs by parsing minimum and maximum values, applying default values and ensuring the min is not less than zero. I\'m focusing on how the validation and transformation of inputs work within this function. The formatting of the `budgetText` field is now clearer.\n\n\n**Examining UI Elements**\n\nMy current focus is on the UI components related to the \"post wanted item\" functionality, specifically in `WantedPlazaView.tsx`. I\'m analyzing the styling of the floating button, making sure it\'s correctly anchored and responsive across devices. I\'m also planning a secondary trigger in the header for easier access. Furthermore, I\'m checking the z-index to ensure the modal renders correctly when the component is open.\n\n\n**Adjusting UI Component Placement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the UI responds when the \"post wanted item\" modal is active. I want to ensure the `BottomNavBar` is hidden, or if visible, doesn\'t interfere with the submit button, even on smaller screens. This involves adjusting the z-index and padding.\n\nI\'m also working on the UI of the \"\345\217\221\345\270\203\346\240\241\345\206\205\346\261\202\350\264\255\351\234\200\346\261\202\" sheet, specifically the header elements, including the title, real-name authentication indicator, and close button. I\'m building out a \"\344\270\200\351\224\256\345\241\253\345\205\245\345\273\272\351\231\242\346\261\202\350\264\255\346\250\241\347\211\210\" section with preset chips. I\'m focusing on defining the properties for each preset chip.\n\n\n**Categorizing Example Data**\n\nI\'m now delving into the data, specifically the example wanted items. I\'m carefully analyzing and grouping these examples based on their categories, titles, descriptions, and budget ranges. I am currently examining location and departmental details within the examples, to improve data organization, and identify common patterns in the wanted items. I am evaluating a set of 4 interactive pills, that allow easy selection of the major categories.\n\n\n**Designing Input Forms**\n\nI\'m now in the design phase, and starting with the input forms themselves. I\'m focusing on the input fields, placeholders, and validation for item names. Specifically, I\'m defining the range of budget input options (dual inputs plus chips), the layout for desired pick-up locations and departments (with chips), and the design of the quick tags area. The goal is to maximize user-friendliness and information clarity.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_10796"
